
  [1mThe reconcile rule[0m, which is where the account-switch protection lives: if the ID we passed in was non-null and the ID
  that came back differs, a [3mdifferent person[0m has logged in on this browser. Clear all stored per-user state first, then
  write the new ID. Today "all per-user state" is exactly one key, so clearing is technically redundant with
  overwriting — but writing the rule this way is what keeps it correct the first time someone stores a second per-user
  value next to it.

  [1mA known gap I don't want to paper over:[0m this clears on account [3mswitch[0m, not on logout — there is no logout in this app.
  A user who walks away from a shared browser leaves their ID behind. Given the ID is non-authoritative that's a
  privacy wrinkle rather than a security hole, but it's a real gap and the spec will say so rather than imply the
  lifecycle is fully handled.

  [1mThe one open decision.[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub with no backend, so there is no real ID for it to return. Two ways to go, and
  they differ in whether this feature is observable at all before an API exists:

  - [1mStub synthesizes a marked placeholder[0m — returns something like [38;5;153mstub-<username>[39m. The full loop runs: first login
    stores an ID, the next one passes it back, a different username triggers the reconcile path. You can see it work and
    it's testable today. Cost: a fake value lives in the store until the endpoint is wired, and the [38;5;153mstub-[39m prefix is
    what makes it obvious if it ever surfaces somewhere it shouldn't.
  - [1mStub returns no ID[0m — nothing is ever stored, [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m always returns [38;5;153mnull[39m, and [38;5;153mlogin[39m always receives [38;5;153mnull[39m.
    Strictly honest, but the store, the reconcile rule, and the parameter are all dead code until a backend lands, and
    none of it can be demonstrated or tested.

  I recommend the placeholder. Untestable-until-later code is where bugs wait.
